    "slot_value": "Use an all-at-once rollback for the scheduler policy itself when the issue is with the scheduling policy and the safest move is to remove the bad behavior immediately across the board. Temporary exceptions are appropriate only for critical jobs with hard deadlines, customer commitments, or safety/availability implications that would be worse off if they waited for the full revert. Any exceptions should be narrowly scoped, time-limited, explicitly approved, documented with the reason and authorizer, and handled under the restored baseline policy as much as possible rather than creating a separate long-term rollback path. Customer commitment cases should be treated as high priority, but not as an unconditional override; if a customer-facing deadline is at risk, the customer owner or account manager must be informed right away and the operations team should be aware so they can decide whether to grant an exception or adjust allocation. The scheduler should prefer meeting the commitment only when it can do so without causing broader policy violations or starving other critical work. Such cases should be explicitly marked, tracked, and reviewed afterward to verify whether the commitment handling was justified.",
