    "slot_value": "The policy should change when the same kind of complaint recurs across several jobs or from multiple users, especially if logs show similar jobs being treated very differently without a clear policy reason. Consistent misses on runtime predictions, repeated near-deadline failures, or a noticeable skew against one tenant or workload class also justify policy changes. A single complaint by itself is usually not enough to change scheduling behavior unless it exposes a clear bug, a policy violation, or a serious operational issue; otherwise it is treated as one data point until a repeated pattern appears. When capacity is tight, defer nice-to-have optimizations such as aggressive fairness tuning, deep lookahead planning, nonessential rebalancing that costs extra scheduling time, and optional analytics or reporting work if they compete with actually placing jobs. Preserve those lower-priority capabilities when they directly protect an important business outcome, such as preventing a major customer deadline miss or avoiding a long unfair backlog for a paying user; defer the optimization unless it clearly contributes to a near-term commitment or materially reduces user harm.",
