    "slot_value": "When resources or policy conditions change after intake, Libra should keep re-evaluating the job until it can be placed safely or until it is impossible under the stated constraints; if impossible, it must report the failure clearly. It should keep waiting or retrying only for temporary conditions, such as transient capacity shortages or a queue that may clear before the deadline; if the constraints themselves make success impossible, it should fail fast rather than keep churning. For temporary scheduling failures, it should retry only while there is still a realistic path to meet the deadline and stay within budget; stop retrying once the remaining time is too short for the job to run plus a safety margin for queueing or startup delays, or when the estimated cost to keep waiting would push it over the spend limit. If the platform has a retry policy, Libra should follow that too, but the deadline check should win if they conflict. When the margin is too thin, it should stop and report that the job is unlikely to succeed rather than waiting until it is already late. If conditions change while the job is running, Libra should try to adjust the allocation plan so the job can continue under the new reality, as long as that still respects the user’s constraints and operator policy. If it can’t adapt cleanly, it should surface the problem quickly rather than silently drifting away from the approved plan. In practice, it should prefer controlled rescheduling or rebalancing over abrupt interruption unless the operator rules force a preemption or the job can no longer be supported safely. The cutoff for lower-priority scheduler capabilities should be whether running them is likely to change the placement decision in time to protect a deadline or commitment; if they won’t materially improve the chance of placing the job before the deadline, they should be deferred. If the job is close to missing a user-facing commitment and the extra work could realisticall